static size_t
is_codefence(uint8_t *data, size_t size, size_t *width, uint8_t *chr)
{
    size_t i = 0, n = 1;
    uint8_t c;

    /* skipping initial spaces */
    if (size < 3)
        return 0;

    if (data[0] == ' ') { i++;
    if (data[1] == ' ') { i++;
    if (data[2] == ' ') { i++; } } }

    /* looking at the hrule uint8_t */
    c = data[i];
    if (i + 2 >= size || !(c=='~' || c=='`'))
        return 0;

    /* the fence must be that same character */
    while (++i < size && data[i] == c)
        ++n;

    if (n < 3)
        return 0;

    if (width) *width = n;
    if (chr) *chr = c;
    return i;
}
```

The function checks `size < 3` at the start. But after skipping up to 3 spaces, `i` could be 3. Then `c = data[i]` reads at index 3. If `size` is exactly 3, then `i = 3` and `size = 3`, so `data[3]` is out of bounds!

Wait, but the check `if (size < 3) return 0;` means size >= 3. If size is 3 and all three chars are spaces, then `i = 3`, and `data[3]` is out of bounds (valid indices are 0, 1, 2).

